britains vote to leave the European  Union makes one thing very clear Europe  needs to rethink one of its core  principles this is a so-called fourth  freedom meaning free movement of people  in addition to the e use guarantee of  free trade in goods services and capital  a degree of control over immigration  policy needs to be given back to  national governments freedom of movement  is enormously valuable making it easy  for any EU citizen to live and work  anywhere in the EU and it fits with the  e u--'s ambition to build an Ave a  closer Union which is why it's being  granted a kind of sacred status Europe's  leaders insist that the Four Freedoms  are indivisible but freedom of movement  clashes with rising fears over  immigration which are helping to fuel  anti-eu populism not just in Britain but  in many other countries as well recently  the EU has been even less popular in  France one of the architects of the  original project and in Britain the  fears are exaggerated countries like the  UK need immigration to fuel economic  growth but the fears are real and  they're dangerous Europe's ongoing  refugee crisis has made matters worse  voters everywhere feel the government's  are ignoring them this helps explain not  just brexit and the rise of parties like  the National Front in France but Donald  Trump's success in the United States too  we have to stop people from pouring into  our country liberal rules for migration  would still be possible even with some  control given back to national  government but the main thing is to  stifle anti-eu populism by bringing the  contentious issue of immigration back  into the realm of national policy and  Democratic choice  if this had been done before the brexit  vote would likely have gone the other  way is too late to do much about that  but flexibility on the fourth freedom  can help the rest of the EU bend before  it breaks
 

